course: dictionary
lesson: 15063
title: Revisit: going back and looking again
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To revisit something is to go back to it. You can revisit a place you have been to before, or revisit a subject you have already learned. «We revisited the seaside town where Grandma grew up.» The word has a clue inside it: re means again, and visit means go to see.
explain: Teachers often revisit topics so you do not forget them. «Our teacher will revisit fractions on Monday.» The class is not learning fractions for the first time. They are looking at them again.
explain: Writers revisit their work too. «Ava read her story again and revisited the ending to make it better.» She looked at the ending a second time so she could improve it. A good check is to swap in "go back to" or "look at again" and see if the sentence still makes sense.
fact: Revisit is a verb: I revisit, he revisits, we revisited, we are revisiting.
fact: «Revisit» means to go back to a place you have been to before.
fact: «Revisit» can also mean to look at or think about a subject again, often to change or improve it.
fact: In stories, Mole goes back to Mole End at Christmas, so he revisits his old home.
fact: The prefix re- means again, so revisit means to visit again.
exercise: «The family will revisit the castle next summer.» What does revisit mean here? | To forget the castle completely | To paint a picture of the sea | To build a new castle from scratch | *To go back to a place they have been to before
exercise: «Mr Patel will revisit the rules of the game before we start.» What does revisit mean here? | To learn a brand-new game | To break the rules on purpose | To throw the rules away | *To look at the rules again
exercise: The prefix re- in revisit means what? | Away | *Again | Never | Under
test: «After ten years, Sam revisited the village where he was born.» What did Sam do? | He moved to a new village far away | He never went to the village again | *He went back to a village he had been to before | He drew a map of the sea
test: «Our teacher wants us to revisit our story and make the ending better.» What should you do with your story? | Post it to a friend | *Look at it again and improve it | Read it once and then stop | Throw it away and start with an empty page
exam: Which sentence uses revisit correctly? | Next week we will revisit the sandwich we are eating now, quickly | Next week revisit we the bus to school | *Next week we will revisit the poem we learned in June | Next week the rain will revisit the moon
